module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BelowCeiling, FoundBelowTheLadder, RecurringFailureMode, RungRead }
import gunbc.rung_climb { AwaitsOneGrounding, GroundingUndeclared }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { StructurallyGuaranteed }

data regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "regen_candidate_reverts_hand_authored_mirror" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead { found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder, standing: BelowCeiling { current: FoundBelowTheLadder, ceiling: StructurallyGuaranteed, blocker: AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: GroundingUndeclared }, trigger_capability: "Refuse BEFORE the base-mirror restore when the worktree holds uncommitted stage0 content, or stage0 content newer than the round's validated base commit, that the round does not author. Proposed; unbuilt. Its ceiling needs a different grounding than this refusal: see the 2026-11-07 receipts." } },

  receipts: [
    "a regeneration installs over a hand-authored file and reports success (THE CANDIDATE TREE CARRIES EVERY MIRROR FILE, INCLUDING THE ONES REGENERATION DOES NOT AUTHOR, so installing the candidate wholesale reverts hand edits to those files while every figure the run prints stays green.",

    "Invalid state: an install step whose population is `every file in the candidate` over a directory that holds BOTH generated and hand-authored files. Harm: hand-authored rows silently disappear -- not only the author's own, but rows other lanes landed earlier, because the candidate's copy is as old as the last build that produced it. The run then reports first_generation_equal and the author reports the verification they genuinely performed BEFORE the install.",

    "THE OTHER DIRECTION OF THE SAME CONFUSION, gunbc#11291: not a regeneration reverting a hand edit, but an AUTHOR hand-editing a file the regeneration owns, which the drift phase then refuses. Recorded here rather than as a neighbour because both arise from one unresolved question -- WHICH SIDE AUTHORS THIS FILE -- and a reader who has internalised only the revert direction still reaches for the editor when the build breaks.",

    "THE MECHANISM, AND WHY IT LOOKED NECESSARY AT THE TIME. Deleting a host seam removed its .dag roster row and its interpreter family arm, while the COMMITTED generated dispatch file still carried the arm's enum variant. The match went non-exhaustive, so the tree would not build -- and with no binary from that tree, there was apparently nothing to regenerate with. The editor looked like the only move. It was not: the PREVIOUS generation's binary, built before the deletion, reads the NEW .dag sources and emits the new mirrors without any edit. A previous-generation seed regenerating the next generation is the bootstrap itself, and the appearance of deadlock came from looking only at the tree that would not build.",

    "THE TELL IS THE AUTHOR'S OWN STATE, NOT THE DIFF, which is what makes this worth a receipt: noticing that you are routing around the build IS the line-stop signal, and the author did not stop. gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed names the workaround as an absorbing fallback executed by the author, and its concealed deficit is usually in the layer the author is least equipped to inspect -- here the bootstrap order. The second instance in the same change had no deadlock at all: a projection mirror was hand-written for a NEW primitive, and the only difference from the emitted candidate turned out to be ROW ORDER, the emitter following the roster order the author had not matched. Same reach for the editor, no obstacle to justify it.",

    "THE DISCRIMINATOR A REVIEWER CAN APPLY WITHOUT READING THE DIFF, offered because a fixed point is what made this one hard to see: a regeneration that AGREES with a hand edit proves the bytes are right and proves nothing about the method, so `first_generation_equal=true` is not evidence the file was authored correctly. Ask instead where the bytes came from. The receipt a generated surface owes is `installed from the candidate emitted by seed X`, and `hand-edited, later confirmed by regeneration` is a different claim wearing the same green.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable.",

    "RECEIPT, gunbc#10688. Two `NAMESPACE_TRANSITION_ADMISSIONS` rows were authored in `src/v1/stage0/src/namespace_wave_admission.rs`, the phase was run, and it reported both ADMITTED-BY. The regen loop then installed the candidate, which reverted that file to a state predating gunbc#10676 -- deleting the two new rows AND the nine gunbc#10676 rows that had been on main for a day. The commit landed with a message asserting the phase green over bytes that no longer carried the admissions, and `git show --stat` on that commit read 23 insertions / 118 DELETIONS in a file the change was supposed to grow.",

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW, and it is not inattention: the verification is REAL and it is simply taken at the wrong moment. Build, run the phase, read green, install, commit -- the install sits between the evidence and the artifact, and nothing in the loop's output mentions the file. A second run would have caught it, which is exactly why a loop that ends in an install must not be the last step.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE: when an install step copies a produced tree over a source tree, ask which files in the DESTINATION are not authored by the producer. Any such file is reverted on every install, and the revert is silent because the producer never claimed to own it. The tell in the log is the absence of a tell -- the file is not named on either side.",

    "A SECOND RULE, and it is the cheaper one: after any generate-and-install loop, read `git diff --stat` for NEGATIVE line counts on files the change was supposed to grow. A net deletion in a file you added rows to is the signature, and it is visible without knowing anything about the mechanism.",

    "CEILING: 3, structurally guaranteed. The generator KNOWS which files it emitted -- it adjudicates them one by one -- so an install whose population is the emitted set rather than the candidate directory cannot touch a file the generator did not author, and no exclusion list is needed. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, a capability: the install step consuming the regeneration's own per-file adjudication roster as its population, so an unadjudicated file in the candidate is not installable rather than being skipped by name.",

    "RECEIPT, THE HAND-EDIT DIRECTION, gunbc#15133 then gunbc#15080 (2026-10-25). gunbc#15133 hand-wrote the test `service_root_binds_only_where_its_service_is_imported` into `src/v1/stage0/src/compiler_tests.rs`. That file is emitted from `v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust`, and the test had no row there. The pull-request lanes regenerate no stage0 mirror, so it landed. The next heal round to touch the generation inputs was on an UNRELATED pull request, gunbc#15080. It regenerated the mirror from the seed's own source and pushed a bot commit deleting the test. That test is the one discriminating RED behind the service-root receipt of `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode` `binding_chosen_by_pool_membership_rather_than_by_the_declared_rule`, so a rung's evidence vanished inside a chore commit on someone else's change, and a reviewer of that change caught it (review 86252). THE REPAIR is the authority, not the mirror: gunbc#15178 (merged as c891c877545329173f548a46c1ecd66dce81753b) makes the test a row of `v1.compiler.compiler_tests_rust` (`ct_service_root_binds_only_where_its_service_is_imported_test`), so regeneration emits it, and gunbc#15080 carries no copy of that row. THE TELL, for a heal receipt: a bot regeneration commit with net deletions in a TEST file is evidence being removed, never drift being repaired.",

    "WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: nothing in the repository was repaired by this row. The actuator that exhibited it is a local loop script, so the mitigation was an exclusion list of file NAMES -- which is the maintained-roster shape this repository files elsewhere, and it is wrong for the same reason: the next hand-authored mirror file is reverted until someone remembers to add it. The class stands at rung 1.)",

    "SPECIMEN, work item adhoc-d9a8b550-3f7 (observed 2026-11-06 06:18Z, recorded 2026-11-07; nothing was built or run for this record). A base-mirror regen round — `claim_executor --regen-round-cost --base-mirror` with `HEAL_BASE_MIRROR_COMMIT` = 3bb5cbff9dad86fec9636a6f5ba82a675b504e66, a commit of 2026-11-05 16:09:41Z — run in a single-branch clone the operator intended to keep, where the base predated the operator's own stage0 mirror work. The round exited 0 and left ` M` on 11 files under `src/v1/stage0/`, worst file `v1_compiler_infer.rs` at 26 insertions / 380 deletions, with four symbols the committed HEAD carried nine times reduced to zero occurrences. The file was not named on either side; an ad-hoc content grep by the operator is the only thing that caught it. This is the same install-wholesale invalid state this row names, reached from the other side: the population installed is not the candidate directory but the BASE COMMIT'S TREE.",

    "MECHANISM, source-supported and NOT confirmed — stated with its confirmation gap so the row does not overclaim. `bootstrap_round_one_from_base_mirror` (`src/v1/stage0/src/required_regen_host.rs:4810`) restores base's ENTIRE tracked population for the crate — its own words at :4818-:4824, `RESTORE THE EXACT VALIDATED BASE POPULATION: every path the base commit tracks under the crate, with NO filter of any kind` — over `STAGE0_CRATE_REL = src/v1/stage0` (:3422), and it journals head-only paths `AbsentBeforeInstall` so the round's own teardown removes them (:4826-:4829, executed by `restore_regen_convergence_journal_for_subject` at :4381-:4393); exit 0 with a dirty tree is the mode's documented outcome, stated in `run_regen_round_cost` at :8560-:8566. Confirmation is UNAVAILABLE, not unattempted: the round-time head `cca9623369be` (`family-2 gate narrowed to the body's own structure`, committed 2026-11-06 06:18:03Z, one second before the round launched) is unreachable and pruned, so the discriminating `git diff --numstat <head> 3bb5cbff… -- src/v1/stage0` cannot be run; the surviving `target/regen-convergence-journal/journal.json` (09:01/09:18Z, starting_commit 334f4ed15de4117e, schema gunbc.regen_convergence_transaction.v1, `PresentBeforeInstall` backups) and `regen-convergence-transaction.json` (07:23Z, starting_commit 04cc75e9287184d3) both post-date the damaged round with foreign starting_commits; and the round's own pre-clean covered the real journal path returned by `regen_convergence_journal_path` (`workspace.join(target/regen-convergence-journal)`, :4207), so a stale-journal restore is excluded. The single unexplained artifact is a `fatal: ambiguous argument 'main'…` line printed mid-round by a caller that then tolerated it — filed as a `gunbc.integration_concern` `SuspectedCapabilityGap` rather than explained here.",

    "CLASS RE-READ 2026-11-07: the typed rung field now records FoundBelowTheLadder for the class. It supersedes the earlier receipt's `RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable`, because the class admits silent members (this specimen: exit 0, dirty tree, caught only by a manual grep), and a class is read at its worst admitted member (gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder rung honesty). The earlier receipt is kept unedited as the record of its specimen. WHY THIS MEMBER SITS BELOW THE LADDER. In the specimens above a `git diff --stat` negative count and a rollback exist, so the harm is containable and those receipts read `RUNG FOUND AT: 1, mitigatable`. Here the reversion is the mode's DOCUMENTED behavior, the run prints success, no figure it prints mentions the file, and the only tell was an author's ad-hoc grep: silent wrongness, which gunbc.design_argument::guarantee_ladder places below the ladder. The row's `rung` records that lower reading and is the single standing claim for the class; the earlier rung prose is kept unedited as the record of its own specimen, not as a competing reading. CEILING AND GROUNDING: `StructurallyGuaranteed` is the ceiling the `CEILING: 3` receipt already argues — the generator knows which files it emitted — but for the RESTORE direction it needs a grounding that does not exist today, which is why the blocker is `AwaitsOneGrounding { grounding: GroundingUndeclared }` rather than `ClimbableButUnbuilt`. No declaration states which paths under `src/v1/stage0` are generated: `.gitattributes` carries 43 `merge=generated-artifact` paths and none under `src/v1/stage0` (`v1_compiler_infer` appears nowhere in it); `gunbc.stage0.stage0_crate_layout_generated` enumerates the emitted crate layout, not the mirror family; and `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.mirror_population_exceeds_install_ownership` already concedes the installed shell observation is still a host read. The grounding, when someone authors it, is a declared per-path authorship for the mirror family — extend the generated-artifact attribute population to it, or derive the mirror population from the compile population and join it positively the way `rebuild_scope_owners_of_mirror` and `HostShellEmittedOwner` do, with the removed-owner mutation witness `live_module_mirrors_have_owners_and_removed_shell_owner_refuses` as the proof shape. With that, an install or restore population restricted to generated paths makes `a round overwrites content it does not author` unrepresentable rather than refused.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
